A||||5||Gokart||Deck lid weatherstrip.||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||03-15-2000||08:04 PM||||Noticed my parts car never had the recalls done! It still has the rubber weatherstrip along the back of the decklid that prevents water from getting on the manifolds. Don't see how this thing caught fire unless the engine itself was on fire then spread to it. I want to put it on my GT! Would you? Noticed someone was selling one at e-bay a couple of weeks ago cause they must be hard to get.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||03-15-2000||08:17 PM||||The reason I heard for its removal during the recall was that it let the gap between the trunk lid and the back window open and allowed a little more air to pass out of the engine compartment. I don't know if it's true or not but it sounds dumb enough to be true.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||03-15-2000||08:17 PM||||I would like to put one on my Fiero. Just haven't done it yet. I don't see the problem either, I think GM just over-reacted. Plus, it keeps the firewall side "manifold" dry.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mutfish||03-15-2000||08:21 PM||||I think it would be a good idea,I replaced the starter on my '88 2.8,and in a couple of months it looked like it was 100 years old...||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||03-15-2000||10:34 PM||||Yeh...me too!<BR>Just changed my plugs, and by the rusty bodies, and the water marks on the shields, the plug hole must fill with water every time it rains or snows.  Can't do the exhaust manifolds any good either.<BR>I've been toying with the idea of fitting an aluminum strip to the original spring clips, possibly with a rubber bumper on the edge against the glass. Anyone know if this type of stuff is available?<BR>Also, I hate that fibreglass that sticks out from beneath the rear glass. I suspect it wicks up the moisture too, as the steel parts that support the glass are rusting.  Anyone got a solution?<BR>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gokart||03-16-2000||05:48 AM||||Thanks guys. I guess that settles it. It's going in this weekend! I had cut a long 3" wide piece of lead and used the holes that are already on the decklid for the clips that held the rubber piece and used small screws to hold it in place, but it bends easily so it doesn't do the job the rubber one will. Looked tacky when you look down in that gap as well. Not enough air getting in there won't be an issue soon when I get the trunk scoop on!||||reg||14
